 ## Overview
-GPU Voxelization via Compute Shaders converts a triangle mesh into a voxel grid by offloading the geometric intersection and occupancy logic to parallel compute kernels. Unlike rasterization-based methods that project geometry into a grid [908-rasterization-based-voxelization.md], this approach explicitly iterates over triangles (or voxels) and uses geometric tests to determine occupancy [S1, unofficial][S3]. It distinguishes itself from [GPU Voxelization](2633-gpu-voxelization.md) by separating surface generation (front/back facing) and interior filling into distinct compute dispatches, allowing for precise control over surface attributes and solid filling [S3].
+GPU Voxelization via Compute Shaders converts a triangle mesh into a voxel grid by offloading geometric intersection and occupancy logic to parallel compute kernels [S1, unofficial][S3]. It distinguishes itself from [GPU Voxelization](2633-gpu-voxelization.md) by separating surface generation (front/back facing) and interior filling into distinct compute dispatches, allowing for precise control over surface attributes and solid filling [S3].
 
 ## How it works
-The method implements a two-phase or three-phase pipeline depending on the specific variant (AHD vs. Boxelizer), but generally follows a pattern of resource preparation, surface voxel generation via compute shaders, and optional interior filling.
